      "side": 1,
      "message": "Sorry for delayed response.\nI have explored this possibility, unfortunately we would need to define a new binding, if we use existing one [intel,pd-altmode.yaml](https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/ec/+/refs/heads/main/zephyr/dts/bindings/usbc/pd/intel%2Cpd-altmode.yaml), we would end up having to define length on each node definition:\n```\n\tpd_port0: ccg@50 {\n\t\tcompatible \u003d \"intel,pd-altmode\";\n\t\treg \u003d \u003c0x50\u003e;\n\t\tstatus-reg-length \u003d \u003clength\u003e;\n\t\tcontrol-reg-length \u003d \u003clength\u003e;\n\t\tirq-gpios \u003d \u003c\u0026gpio9 5 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW\u003e;\n\t};\n\tpd_port1: ccg@51 {\n\t\tcompatible \u003d \"intel,pd-altmode\";\n\t\treg \u003d \u003c0x51\u003e;\n\t\tstatus-reg-length \u003d \u003clength\u003e;\n\t\tcontrol-reg-length \u003d \u003clength\u003e;\n\t\tirq-gpios \u003d \u003c\u0026gpio9 5 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW\u003e;\n\t\tirq-shared;\n\t};\n```\nThis would be error prone.\n\nSince these status registers lengths are more tied to SOC type, it would be better to vary them based on that, example:\n```\n#if defined(CONFIG_AP_X86_INTEL_MTL)\nINTEL_ALTMODE_DATA_STATUS_REG_LEN 5\n#elif define(\u003cINTEL SOC TYPE\u003e)\n#else\nINTEL_ALTMODE_DATA_STATUS_REG_LEN 4\n#endif\n```",
